<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we formulated controllable 3D scene authoring as incremental requirement satisfaction and introduced <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MUSE</span>, a memory-grounded multi-agent framework for requirement compilation, iterative execution, and step-wise verification. Together with AuthorBench, a requirement-level benchmark for construction and preservation-aware editing, our experiments show that <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MUSE</span> outperforms one-shot baselines in dense construction and mitigates the editing completion-locality trade-off, achieving strong task completion with near-perfect structural stability (PR 99.9%, UCR 0.6%). Human evaluations and downstream navigation-proxy tests further support its alignment with user intent and spatial stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Limitation.</span> <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MUSE</span> and AuthorBench are currently limited to indoor scenes and single-instruction edits, and <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MUSE</span> still relies on repeated LLM-guided verification. Future work will extend the domain scope, distill verification into efficient models, and support long-horizon multi-turn authoring. These results point toward a shift from single-shot holistic generation to verifiable, requirement-level 3D scene progression.</p>
</div>
</section>
